1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,546
Welcome to Four Eyes Report.
This week: Amazing K-defense.

2
00:00:05,546 --> 00:00:07,460
And then, an alphabet.

3
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:11,650
Amazing K-defense. That
sounds like a movie trailer.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,847
The numbers are real, though.

5
00:00:13,847 --> 00:00:18,558
In twenty twenty-five, Korea was the
world's fourth-largest arms exporter,

6
00:00:18,558 --> 00:00:21,360
with about six percent
of the global market.

7
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:24,590
Fourth in the world? That's
higher than I guessed.

8
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:29,420
And it's more than four times
what Korea exported ten years ago.

9
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:32,780
About twenty point eight
trillion won last year.

10
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:36,650
And this week came a
new chapter. Ms. Nonfic.

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,204
Good evening. On October eighth,

12
00:00:39,204 --> 00:00:44,094
South Korea test-launched its first
hypersonic glide vehicle, or H G V,

13
00:00:44,094 --> 00:00:47,400
developed by the Agency
for Defense Development.

14
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:52,150
Hypersonic glide vehicle. That's
three words I need explained.

15
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:57,173
Hypersonic means at least five
times the speed of sound. Mach five.

16
00:00:57,173 --> 00:01:00,660
About six thousand one
hundred kilometers an hour.

17
00:01:01,010 --> 00:01:04,450
Wait. Don't ballistic
missiles already go that fast?

18
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:07,535
Many do. The difference is the path.

19
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:12,853
A ballistic missile flies a high
arc, up and down, like a thrown ball.

20
00:01:12,853 --> 00:01:15,360
You can calculate where it lands.

21
00:01:15,710 --> 00:01:16,350
And the glider?

22
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,021
A rocket booster carries it high up.

23
00:01:19,021 --> 00:01:23,855
Then it separates and glides down
through the atmosphere at lower altitude,

24
00:01:23,855 --> 00:01:25,660
and it can change direction.

25
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:28,896
A thrown ball versus a paper airplane.

26
00:01:28,896 --> 00:01:32,010
A very fast, very serious paper airplane.

27
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:37,160
I would not use the term paper
airplane. But the physics is not wrong.

28
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:39,670
Why is that so hard to stop?

29
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:40,987
Three reasons.

30
00:01:40,987 --> 00:01:46,235
One: it flies low, and the curve of the
Earth is in the way of ground radar,

31
00:01:46,235 --> 00:01:48,100
so it can be spotted later.

32
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:53,152
Two: missile defenses predict where
a target will be, then aim there.

33
00:01:53,152 --> 00:01:57,650
If the target keeps changing course,
the prediction keeps missing.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,375
Three: less time.

35
00:01:59,375 --> 00:02:05,280
By the time it is seen, the window to
respond can be a matter of minutes.

36
00:02:05,630 --> 00:02:07,150
So it's impossible to stop?

37
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:11,882
Nobody serious says
impossible. They say difficult.

38
00:02:11,882 --> 00:02:14,460
I prefer accurate to dramatic.

39
00:02:14,810 --> 00:02:16,570
And what's hard about building one?

40
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:18,480
Heat. At these speeds,

41
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:23,302
friction with the air heats the
surface far past a thousand degrees.

42
00:02:23,302 --> 00:02:26,280
It needs special heat-resistant materials.

43
00:02:26,630 --> 00:02:28,539
And a communication problem.

44
00:02:28,539 --> 00:02:32,425
The heat can turn the air
around the vehicle into plasma,

45
00:02:32,425 --> 00:02:34,470
which can block radio signals.

46
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:37,460
So it's flying fast and can't call home.

47
00:02:37,810 --> 00:02:39,913
So what did Korea's test check?

48
00:02:39,913 --> 00:02:43,711
Officials listed flight path
and speed, heat protection,

49
00:02:43,711 --> 00:02:47,170
guidance and control, and
low-altitude maneuvering.

50
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:51,985
The presidential office says the
vehicle separated from its booster,

51
00:02:51,985 --> 00:02:53,692
followed its planned path,

52
00:02:53,692 --> 00:02:57,632
adjusted course horizontally
and vertically at low altitude,

53
00:02:57,632 --> 00:02:58,880
and hit its target.

54
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:01,779
Speed and range were not disclosed.

55
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:05,055
The site was not officially
disclosed either.

56
00:03:05,055 --> 00:03:08,696
Yonhap reported a barge
at a test center in Taean.

57
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:11,390
That part is reported, not confirmed.

58
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:15,740
"Reported, not confirmed." That's
Ms. Nonfic's love language.

59
00:03:16,090 --> 00:03:19,551
President Lee Jae Myung attended
with the defense minister.

60
00:03:19,551 --> 00:03:25,770
He said the government will fully support the development
and operational deployment of advanced weaponry.

61
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:29,476
He also said the team must
not rest on today's success,

62
00:03:29,476 --> 00:03:33,320
and that national security should
rest on Korea's own strength.

63
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:36,234
The same day, he inspected the Hyunmoo-5,

64
00:03:36,234 --> 00:03:39,925
a surface-to-surface missile
nicknamed the monster missile.

65
00:03:39,925 --> 00:03:43,990
Officials used that name in a
press statement for the first time.

66
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:48,100
The monster missile. The naming
department had a good day.

67
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:50,997
The timing matters. On October third,

68
00:03:50,997 --> 00:03:57,193
North Korea test-fired what Seoul assessed as
a hypersonic medium-range ballistic missile.

69
00:03:57,193 --> 00:04:00,498
Seoul put its range at
seven hundred kilometers.

70
00:04:00,498 --> 00:04:02,770
North Korea claimed one thousand.

71
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:04,160
So it's a race.

72
00:04:04,510 --> 00:04:07,950
I would not call it a race.
I would call it a pattern.

73
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:11,180
Is Korea building anything
else in this area?

74
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:16,202
Yes. HyCore. It is a different
type: a hypersonic cruise missile.

75
00:04:16,202 --> 00:04:20,514
It keeps its engine running the
whole way, using a scramjet.

76
00:04:20,514 --> 00:04:24,970
ADD and Hyundai Rotem have worked
on it since twenty eighteen.

77
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:27,160
Scramjet. What's that?

78
00:04:27,510 --> 00:04:31,925
An engine that burns fuel in air
rushing past faster than sound.

79
00:04:31,925 --> 00:04:35,030
Think of keeping a candle
lit in a hurricane.

80
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:37,201
In a twenty twenty-four test,

81
00:04:37,201 --> 00:04:41,282
HyCore passed Mach six at an
altitude of twenty-three kilometers.

82
00:04:41,282 --> 00:04:45,300
The partners aim to begin mass
production by twenty thirty-five.

83
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:49,170
Twenty thirty-five. So the
future is nine years away.

84
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:53,968
I will update the facts as they
change. I do not update opinions.

85
00:04:53,968 --> 00:04:55,200
I do not have any.

86
00:04:56,650 --> 00:05:02,661
Last story, a softer one. Last
Friday, October ninth, was Hangul Day.

87
00:05:02,661 --> 00:05:04,490
Our historian, Dr. H.

88
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:07,851
Last time, I told you who made Hangul.

89
00:05:07,851 --> 00:05:13,240
This time, the holiday itself has
a birthday. It turned one hundred.

90
00:05:13,590 --> 00:05:16,470
The holiday is a hundred?
Who threw the first party?

91
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:18,333
In nineteen twenty-six,

92
00:05:18,333 --> 00:05:21,952
the Korean Language Society
held the first celebration,

93
00:05:21,952 --> 00:05:26,820
for the four hundred eightieth anniversary
of the fourteen forty-six text.

94
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:28,130
What was it called?

95
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:36,264
"Gagya Day." It comes from the opening
syllables of the Korean alphabet chart:

96
00:05:36,264 --> 00:05:38,160
ga, gya, geo, gyeo.

97
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:42,830
So it was literally Alphabet
Day. Like "A-B-C Day."

98
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:47,652
Two years later, in nineteen
twenty-eight, it was renamed Hangul Day.

99
00:05:47,652 --> 00:05:52,060
And the date moved several times
before it settled on October ninth.

100
00:05:52,410 --> 00:05:55,298
It lost its day off in the early nineties.

101
00:05:55,298 --> 00:05:59,837
In the mid-two-thousands it became
an official national day again,

102
00:05:59,837 --> 00:06:04,650
but still without a day off. The day
off came back in twenty thirteen.

103
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,640
A holiday that got laid off and rehired.

104
00:06:07,990 --> 00:06:12,504
Why is it in the news? Because
of foreign learners. This week,

105
00:06:12,504 --> 00:06:20,150
the King Sejong Institute Foundation invited a record
three hundred fifty-seven Korean learners to Korea.

106
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:24,222
A hundred eighty-one of them came
to a speaking and writing contest,

107
00:06:24,222 --> 00:06:25,700
from seventy-one countries.

108
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:30,210
Seventy-one countries. That's more
than I've visited, even in my head.

109
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:35,360
The speaking contest had about two hundred
eighty-four applicants for every finalist spot.

110
00:06:35,710 --> 00:06:37,710
That's harder than
getting concert tickets.

111
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:43,004
The grand prize in speaking
went to Marjan Haghi of Iran.

112
00:06:43,004 --> 00:06:47,340
In writing, it went to
Nguyen Bao Chau of Vietnam.

113
00:06:47,690 --> 00:06:52,570
The writing finalists wrote their
essays wearing Joseon-era scholar robes.

114
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:55,080
Finally, a dress code for homework.

115
00:06:55,430 --> 00:06:57,275
Why do they learn Korean?

116
00:06:57,275 --> 00:07:02,883
A King Sejong Institute survey found
"Korean content, such as K-pop, dramas,

117
00:07:02,883 --> 00:07:07,753
and movies" was the top reason,
at fifty-three point four percent.

118
00:07:07,753 --> 00:07:11,590
It was the first time that
answer ranked number one.

119
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:14,580
So the idol songs were
homework in disguise.

120
00:07:14,930 --> 00:07:18,406
One learner from Cairo,
Amira Kamal Mohamed,

121
00:07:18,406 --> 00:07:24,016
started with the K-pop group EXO.
She has studied Korean for ten years.

122
00:07:24,016 --> 00:07:27,650
She said it feels like
she's inside a K-drama.

123
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,855
A learner from Canada, who started
with K-pop in twenty nineteen,

124
00:07:31,855 --> 00:07:36,480
said that being able to read it makes
her feel her Korean has really improved.

125
00:07:36,830 --> 00:07:38,570
Reading is the real level-up.

126
00:07:38,570 --> 00:07:42,110
The first time I read a menu,
I felt like I'd won a trophy.

127
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:44,073
And it's not only fandom.

128
00:07:44,073 --> 00:07:52,200
A foundation survey in twenty twenty-five found fifty point nine
percent of foreign learners were preparing to study in Korea.

129
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:54,780
Thirty-seven percent cited career goals.

130
00:07:55,130 --> 00:07:57,770
So it's not just a crush. It's a plan.

131
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:00,450
And that's the point of Hangul.

132
00:08:00,450 --> 00:08:04,433
King Sejong wanted ordinary
people to read and write.

133
00:08:04,433 --> 00:08:08,867
Five hundred eighty years later,
they're still learning it,

134
00:08:08,867 --> 00:08:10,520
sometimes from a song.

135
00:08:10,870 --> 00:08:14,790
Let's wrap up. K-defense on
the rise, and an alphabet.

136
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:16,260
Core, what's your take?

137
00:08:16,610 --> 00:08:20,504
My take: both stories are about
things built to be useful.

138
00:08:20,504 --> 00:08:24,130
A weapon that works, and an
alphabet anyone can learn.

139
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:25,200
And the missile?

140
00:08:25,550 --> 00:08:29,470
I'll leave that one to Ms.
Nonfic. She doesn't have opinions.

141
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:30,780
And the alphabet?

142
00:08:31,130 --> 00:08:32,332
The alphabet wins.

143
00:08:32,332 --> 00:08:36,674
One hundred years of a holiday,
and people are still learning it.

144
00:08:36,674 --> 00:08:38,010
Thanks for watching.
